        {
          "id": 2746169612,
          "type": "DiscussionNote",
          "body": "Would users downloading Wireshark to install 1) prefer a name that has spaces, 2) prefer a name that doesn't have spaces, or 3) not care?",
          "author": {
            "id": 5636682,
            "username": "guyharris",
            "public_email": "",
            "name": "Guy Harris",
            "state": "active",
            "locked": false,
            "avatar_url": "https://secure.gravatar.com/avatar/1b7c243a9b1d6587a9a9067e5d83aba1e87212df7c4eeae3690f791ecbe4675a?s=80&d=identicon",
            "web_url": "https://gitlab.com/guyharris"
          },
          "created_at": "2025-09-11T20:47:42.306Z",
          "updated_at": "2025-09-11T20:47:42.306Z",
          "system": false,
          "noteable_id": 414507571,
          "noteable_type": "MergeRequest",
          "project_id": 7898047,
          "resolvable": true,
          "resolved": false,
          "resolved_by": null,
          "resolved_at": null,
          "confidential": false,
          "internal": false,
          "imported": false,
          "imported_from": "none",
          "noteable_iid": 21322,
          "commands_changes": {}
        },
        {
          "id": 2746178833,
          "type": "DiscussionNote",
          "body": "> There is a couple of commands that that are executed against the .dmg files and it's just annoying needing to escape the spaces \"Wireshark\\\\ 4.7.0..\\\\ Arm\\\\ x64.dmg\"\n\nWhen dealing file names from the command line, I often use file name completion, which escapes spaces for you if they're part of the match.\n\nWhen dealing with file names from scripts/CMake files/Makefiles/etc., since I only have to type the backslash once when entering the name into the file, it's not a big issue for me.",
          "author": {
            "id": 5636682,
            "username": "guyharris",
            "public_email": "",
            "name": "Guy Harris",
            "state": "active",
            "locked": false,
            "avatar_url": "https://secure.gravatar.com/avatar/1b7c243a9b1d6587a9a9067e5d83aba1e87212df7c4eeae3690f791ecbe4675a?s=80&d=identicon",
            "web_url": "https://gitlab.com/guyharris"
          },
          "created_at": "2025-09-11T20:52:22.060Z",
          "updated_at": "2025-09-11T20:52:22.060Z",
          "system": false,
          "noteable_id": 414507571,
          "noteable_type": "MergeRequest",
          "project_id": 7898047,
          "resolvable": true,
          "resolved": false,
          "resolved_by": null,
          "resolved_at": null,
          "confidential": false,
          "internal": false,
          "imported": false,
          "imported_from": "none",
          "noteable_iid": 21322,
          "commands_changes": {}
        },
        {
          "id": 2746398580,
          "type": "DiscussionNote",
          "body": "Yep, shell tab-completion is a massive time-saver (and sanity-saver for complex escaping situations), and I'm only mildly embarrassed by the little happy-dance I did when I realised I could also use wildcards as a shortcut _even if I knew there would only be one match_ (i.e. `Wi*.dmg` if there's only going to be one such file).\n\n@oakimk: What is the workflow where you find you need to perform manual escaping?  I would personally have a mild preference to find out if we can improve that workflow, such as by discovering whether you have options for filename completion with escaping handled for you, rather than replacing the spaces with dashes.",
          "author": {
            "id": 7889756,
            "username": "dariusd0",
            "public_email": "",
            "name": "dariusd0",
            "state": "active",
            "locked": false,
            "avatar_url": "https://secure.gravatar.com/avatar/f0f4a609abaf984c9cfe277f1321b8db104aea7290fcbb7fb0c5749a00d32e39?s=80&d=identicon",
            "web_url": "https://gitlab.com/dariusd0"
          },
          "created_at": "2025-09-12T00:09:39.981Z",
          "updated_at": "2025-09-12T00:58:24.286Z",
          "system": false,
          "noteable_id": 414507571,
          "noteable_type": "MergeRequest",
          "project_id": 7898047,
          "resolvable": true,
          "resolved": false,
          "resolved_by": null,
          "resolved_at": null,
          "confidential": false,
          "internal": false,
          "imported": false,
          "imported_from": "none",
          "noteable_iid": 21322,
          "commands_changes": {}
        },
        {
          "id": 2746429097,
          "type": "DiscussionNote",
          "body": "> Would users downloading Wireshark to install 1) prefer a name that has spaces, 2) prefer a name that doesn't have spaces, or 3) not care?\n\nMy _suspicion_ is that _most_ would not really care, and the number of folks who are human-interface-focussed who'd think more highly of an application that uses human-friendly spaces rather than machine-friendly symbols is probably roughly balanced by the folks who have workflows that run into trouble with spaces in the name and would prefer symbols... (No, I have no statistics whatsoever to back up that claim.)\n\nAnd the Homebrew folks, for instance, might prefer [we left things as-is](https://github.com/Homebrew/homebrew-cask/blob/HEAD/Casks/w/wireshark-app.rb#L39) without necessarily having any stake in the spaces-vs.-dashes debate.\n\nA casual survey of my Mac's `Downloads` folder:\n\n* Uses spaces: Firefox, Wireshark\n* Uses dashes: VMware Fusion, GIMP, Signal, VLC, Skype, Wickr\n* Uses underscores: LibreOffice",
          "author": {
            "id": 7889756,
            "username": "dariusd0",
            "public_email": "",
            "name": "dariusd0",
            "state": "active",
            "locked": false,
            "avatar_url": "https://secure.gravatar.com/avatar/f0f4a609abaf984c9cfe277f1321b8db104aea7290fcbb7fb0c5749a00d32e39?s=80&d=identicon",
            "web_url": "https://gitlab.com/dariusd0"
          },
          "created_at": "2025-09-12T00:40:35.085Z",
          "updated_at": "2025-09-12T00:40:35.085Z",
          "system": false,
          "noteable_id": 414507571,
          "noteable_type": "MergeRequest",
          "project_id": 7898047,
          "resolvable": true,
          "resolved": false,
          "resolved_by": null,
          "resolved_at": null,
          "confidential": false,
          "internal": false,
          "imported": false,
          "imported_from": "none",
          "noteable_iid": 21322,
          "commands_changes": {}
        },
        {
          "id": 2746831359,
          "type": "DiscussionNote",
          "body": "My workflow was pretty much following `.gitlab-ci.yml` but since me being cautious (noob new on mac) I want to use full complete name, and tab-completion does not work. Second, compare to our other installers .exe/rpm/deb none of them has space in the name, I felt this should have been an easy change :smile: \n\nBut as you show we are locked in due to other services now has adopted to the spaces in the name. Ofcourse we could request Homebrew for an update to handle new spaceless name.\n\nThe .dmg I'm creating is for company internal usage, with own codesign of dmg, hence I also meet other problem like codesign without sparkle !21300\n\nPersonlly I prefer dashes, but also in favor of microservices versoning https://semver.org/",
          "author": {
            "id": 6524193,
            "username": "oakimk",
            "public_email": "",
            "name": "Joakim Karlsson",
            "state": "active",
            "locked": false,
            "avatar_url": "https://gitlab.com/uploads/-/system/user/avatar/6524193/avatar.png?v=1787702414",
            "web_url": "https://gitlab.com/oakimk"
          },
          "created_at": "2025-09-12T07:15:27.601Z",
          "updated_at": "2025-09-12T07:15:27.601Z",
